[{:db/id "q-83890/selection/b3c31e0e2d10ac6352946ed6a701a2833119f2aabfabe023adb86915d661b475" :answer/id #uuid "5f910854-9f4d-5a10-b8a9-39c1f06ed416" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "202" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83890/selection/7bf70618a4e6f40057d7165f0879e9c9098ddc7a918d045491f6b733568c894f" :answer/id #uuid "9220e045-52cf-5927-a49d-5ebe280e1360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "173" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83890/selection/88cb40a5aa81536c31e0da9f3fdf8633a9ef2eb2c8ee599244b4420d6d85950d" :answer/id #uuid "cb25506c-8b6d-5ad9-a5e8-2ea6dfe0790e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "289" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83890/selection/f3ff5c998975faa5ae1fc838e2289026609a40c7f3e1f1f8d8bd7b80687253e7" :answer/id #uuid "424ea656-d9e8-5449-a6f1-0f761d89587e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "366" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83890/selection/e3878b7d5a5fe80b0993384dee6d0ec1294bf8b150d34a9a4368f7a61521f6b9" :answer/id #uuid "2ebbcf69-2f81-5012-9af1-3e98cee4ccca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "214" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83890/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4713e061-bb57-5559-ae21-1781cf141215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83890/selection/b3c31e0e2d10ac6352946ed6a701a2833119f2aabfabe023adb86915d661b475" :answer-field/choices ["q-83890/selection/b3c31e0e2d10ac6352946ed6a701a2833119f2aabfabe023adb86915d661b475" "q-83890/selection/7bf70618a4e6f40057d7165f0879e9c9098ddc7a918d045491f6b733568c894f" "q-83890/selection/88cb40a5aa81536c31e0da9f3fdf8633a9ef2eb2c8ee599244b4420d6d85950d" "q-83890/selection/f3ff5c998975faa5ae1fc838e2289026609a40c7f3e1f1f8d8bd7b80687253e7" "q-83890/selection/e3878b7d5a5fe80b0993384dee6d0ec1294bf8b150d34a9a4368f7a61521f6b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83890" :question/id #uuid "a08d81ba-fc21-563c-8e11-eb7caf22c807" :question/math-academy-id "q-83890" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of owls in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P-0.000\\,2{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $100$ owls in the forest initially, how many owls inhabited the forest after $10$ years? Round you answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.1P-0.000\\,2{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,2P(500-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.000\\,2)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =100 \\\\ \\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.1(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =5 \\\\ C & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=10$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =\\frac{500}{1+4{e}^{-0.1(10)}} \\\\  & ≈202.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $10$ years, approximately $202$ owls inhabited the forest." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83890/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83890"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892/selection/d00f482ed129d69c73b9717e729443faf6a8374f48bd82d1581f957a1afe2d04" :answer/id #uuid "9fc57e84-25b2-5ee5-afe7-84ac847340f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "764" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892/selection/0c96064a53134c4c062513c3b0a6390327f7be11ce48138cdbe7166a6ae34ff9" :answer/id #uuid "b84e1eea-da11-5045-bde6-b82632d50a41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1258" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892/selection/abbb2d29dcf0237a2e60bbd16a43b62de13b7357f166084e7163c10895ea9ba0" :answer/id #uuid "94b10ab0-b17d-5e58-a44c-79b2608abd63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1047" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892/selection/bd948ae54d95c470ec75cac11fc55bdad96fa167a2173d88fad74fd0accac40a" :answer/id #uuid "cb40ca50-f770-584c-b0d8-7c52f3730a98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1521" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892/selection/aff85f50ad0f29968d0ec704c1e6c1e20003fd382e5bb3f215f530412827f090" :answer/id #uuid "7b5a396a-9db4-5b09-b754-9297d38c2599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "997" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d863bd-c2d3-51b8-a0da-1526bed583ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83892/selection/abbb2d29dcf0237a2e60bbd16a43b62de13b7357f166084e7163c10895ea9ba0" :answer-field/choices ["q-83892/selection/d00f482ed129d69c73b9717e729443faf6a8374f48bd82d1581f957a1afe2d04" "q-83892/selection/0c96064a53134c4c062513c3b0a6390327f7be11ce48138cdbe7166a6ae34ff9" "q-83892/selection/abbb2d29dcf0237a2e60bbd16a43b62de13b7357f166084e7163c10895ea9ba0" "q-83892/selection/bd948ae54d95c470ec75cac11fc55bdad96fa167a2173d88fad74fd0accac40a" "q-83892/selection/aff85f50ad0f29968d0ec704c1e6c1e20003fd382e5bb3f215f530412827f090"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83892" :question/id #uuid "79a1ac0d-15f0-5ffb-9bf7-5b5bee38a3f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83892" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of students $P(t)$ in a school who have heard a certain rumor follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours, since the rumor started spreading. If there were $2$ students who knew the rumor initially, how many students have heard the rumor after $7$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,5P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(2\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,5)t}}=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{2\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(0)}} & =2 \\\\ 1+C & =1\\,000 \\\\ C & =999\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+999{e}^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =\\frac{2\\,000}{1+999{e}^{-7}} \\\\  & ≈1047.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ hours, approximately $1047$ students have heard the rumor." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83892/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83892"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353254/field-1/0a7584ce423f3dd0a9808fa0dd0c7cf53053fd5a6c514ac2ed1704b14fe1f98a" :answer/id #uuid "11ec8548-8156-5825-8a3e-8c991cce418a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9e^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353254/field-1/7efc7904ce2c8a4cda64cd9bf14f272fd9b7c9e8f7170b35315bc54927ad3186" :answer/id #uuid "bdd18404-6e17-5912-a001-3bf1565a0fc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9e^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353254/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "08604c8c-e1b9-5dee-be5e-c072ca94672c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353254/field-1/0a7584ce423f3dd0a9808fa0dd0c7cf53053fd5a6c514ac2ed1704b14fe1f98a" :answer-field/choices ["q-353254/field-1/0a7584ce423f3dd0a9808fa0dd0c7cf53053fd5a6c514ac2ed1704b14fe1f98a" "q-353254/field-1/7efc7904ce2c8a4cda64cd9bf14f272fd9b7c9e8f7170b35315bc54927ad3186"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353254" :question/id #uuid "d66b0ab0-fd3c-5d87-be7a-bba9eaae950e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353254" :question/problem "The growth rate of the number of students enrolled in an after-school program, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t,$ measured in weeks, and the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.004$ and the maximum enrollment is $500,$ find an expression for the number of students enrolled after $t$ weeks, given that there were $50$ students enrolled initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since the number of students enrolled grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled, the number of students enrolled follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.004,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.004\\,P(500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.004)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=50$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0}} & =50 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{50} \\\\ C & =10-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+9{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353254/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353254"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353256/field-1/3fa8eded6729d8704f3be3dce67cfbe6fa16b2f2fa5275ed6873cb870d6cc62c" :answer/id #uuid "e6224e90-b97d-554c-be3c-039a3fd82461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4e^{-0.5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353256/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6465b12f-27af-5135-ad39-5600febb0324" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353256/field-1/3fa8eded6729d8704f3be3dce67cfbe6fa16b2f2fa5275ed6873cb870d6cc62c" :answer-field/choices ["q-353256/field-1/3fa8eded6729d8704f3be3dce67cfbe6fa16b2f2fa5275ed6873cb870d6cc62c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353256" :question/id #uuid "e8c3a91a-4c08-55c3-b41e-b5dc4f85c448" :question/math-academy-id "q-353256" :question/problem "The growth rate of the number of trees in a reforestation area, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of trees at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the area’s carrying capacity and the number of trees.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $\\frac{1}{3000}$ and the carrying capacity is $1500,$ find an expression for the number of trees after $t$ years, given that there were $300$ trees in the area initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $1500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since the number of trees grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of trees, the number of trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{1}{3000},$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=\\frac{1}{3000}\\,P(1500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-(1500\\cdot \\frac{1}{3000})t}}=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-t/2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=300$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0}} & =300 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1500}{300} \\\\ C & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+4{e}^{-t/2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353256/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353256"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353261/field-1/3099510fbc4bf8147ec0def373dfa1dfd2ac49d99925b0f27ed5cf63a9fb1e2e" :answer/id #uuid "c250200d-0f64-5c10-8ac4-ec5064456705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "393" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353261/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5cd81a5-e2b6-5443-805a-490c398e977d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353261/field-1/3099510fbc4bf8147ec0def373dfa1dfd2ac49d99925b0f27ed5cf63a9fb1e2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-353261/field-1/3099510fbc4bf8147ec0def373dfa1dfd2ac49d99925b0f27ed5cf63a9fb1e2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353261" :question/id #uuid "915582a3-47ae-5196-8aa7-f705f9ce8511" :question/math-academy-id "q-353261" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of customers signed up for a grocery store’s rewards program follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.35P-0.0007{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. If there were $120$ customers signed up initially, how many customers were signed up after $7$ months? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $7$ months, approximately\n{{field-1}} customers were signed up." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.35P-0.0007{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0007P(500-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0007,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.0007)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.35t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=120$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-0.35(0)}} & =120 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{120} \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{6}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{-0.35t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{500}{1+\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{-0.35(7)}} \\\\  & ≈393.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ months, approximately $393$ customers were signed up." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353261/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353261"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
